    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d249">

                <pb facs="0357" n="277" xml:id="pg277"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">501.45A2/435</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The British Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Lindsay</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-05-23">May 23, 1930</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Secretary</hi>: On August 22nd last my
                    predecessor left at the Department of State an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-memoire</hi> on the subject of the draft convention on oil pollution
                    stating that the Embassy had received instructions to enquire whether the United
                    States Government, if officially approached, would be disposed to exert their
                    good offices on behalf of the draft convention with the other governments
                    interested. A copy of this <hi rendition="#italic">aide-memoire</hi> is enclosed
                    for convenience of reference.<anchor n="82" xml:id="d249-f1"/></p>
                <p>As we do not appear to have any record of a reply to this enquiry and as my
                    government have again intimated that they would be glad of one at an early date,
                    I should be grateful for an answer in the near future.</p>
                <p>Believe me [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">R. C. Lindsay</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
